ork/results/s00/claude/20261027_171558/paper/paper_evidence_audit.md\n- figure validation failed: figures[2].source_ids must be non-empty list\n\n## Supplementary Information validation\n- appendix_present: `True`\n- heading_present: `True`\n- placed_after_references: `True`\n- pre_bibliography_clearpage_present: `True`\n- clearpage_present: `True`\n- visible_character_count: `4695`\n- substantive_element_count: `9`\n- rendered_checked: `False`\n- rendered_heading_present: `False`\n- rendered_visible_character_count: `0`\n\n## Evidence validation\n- bundle_path: `/home/user/orca/projects/researchworld-eval/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-s00-20261027T171535Z/work/results/s00/claude/20261027_171558/paper/scientific-evidence`\n- bundle_present: `True`\n- verification_report: `/home/user/orca/projects/researchworld-eval/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-s00-20261027T171535Z/work/results/s00/claude/20261027_171558/paper/scientific-evidence/verification-report.json`\n- total_references: `50`\n- resolved_references: `16`\n- anchored_references: `34`\n- dangling_references: `0`\n- manifests_checked: `4`\n\n## Agentic evidence audit\n- checked: `True`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `2`\n- summary: Numbers, qualification, selection, source-line and protocol claims check out, but two visible statements are unsupported or conflict with the paper's own grounded results.\n\n### Audit findings to fix\n- `intro-bound-tightness-1` (Introduction): No grounded claim supports the word \"only\". It conflicts with the paper's own closed-form analysis, where the cycle (not a bottlenecked graph) has a constant overshoot of exactly ln 4, so the bound is tight up to a factor of 4 at every n.\n  - claim: the bound $2m(n-1)$ is attained up to constant factors only by bottlenecked graphs such as paths and lollipops\n  - evidence: paper.tex Method Design rationale (cycle overshoot \"exactly ln 4 ≈ 1.39 at every n\"); closed_form_gap.json log_overshoot.cycle = 1.3863 for all n; claims_grounding.json has no claim for this statement.\n  - requested_fix: Drop \"only\" and the bottleneck characterisation. For example: \"the bound is tight up to a constant factor on some graphs such as paths, cycles and lollipops, and overshoots by a factor that grows with n on dense graphs (Section~\\ref{sec:rationale}).\"\n- `clip-cap-inconsistency-1` (Discussion and Limitations (Evaluation scope and label noise)): The paper's own grounded derivation shows that a family-macro of 0.181 limits any single graph's log error to at most 24 x 0.181 ≈ 4.35, which is below the clip of 10. The clip therefore cannot have been active, and it cannot hide very large failures in this result. The limitation as written contradicts the earlier worst-case-concentration statement.\n  - claim: the clipped log error saturates at 10, which would hide very large failures. Per-graph errors were not retained, so whether any test graph approaches that cap cannot be checked here.\n  - evidence: paper.tex Results section \"What the evidence does not show\" (no single graph exceeds about 4.35); claims_grounding.json claim[8] (derived from final.metric.macro_log_mae); protocol_facts.json ERR_CLIP=10; evaluate_final.py macro_error clips at ERR_CLIP.\n  - requested_fix: Replace the sentence with a statement that the clip at 10 cannot have been binding, since the macro value bounds every graph's error at about 4.35 (factor about 78). Keep the limitation that per-graph and per-family errors were not retained.\n\n## Soft warnings\n- figures[1].min_font_size_pt below minimum: 7.7 < 8.0\n- figures[1] PNG width too small: 1500 < 2400\n- figures[2].min_font_size_pt below minimum: 7.7 < 8.0\n- figures[2] PNG width too small: 1500 < 2400\n# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. The bibliography has three entries (Feige upper and lower bounds, Kiwi et al. on hyperbolic random graphs). The Feige and Kiwi citations are adequate. Three places in the draft need targeted retrieval. No retrieval was run in this stage.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Aleliunas et al. random-walk cover time bound / universal traversal sequences\n- Lovász survey on random walks on graphs\n- Commute-time identity, effective resistance and Matthews-type cover time bounds\n- Ridge regression (Hoerl and Kennard)\n\n## Rewrite targets\n\n### 1. related-work-worst-case-bounds (high, comparator)\n- **Section:** Related Work (also the Introduction)\n- **Anchor:** \"The $2m(n-1)$ bound of Aleliunas et al. follows by summing commute times ... Lovász's survey on random walks on graphs\"\n- **Reason:** The 2m(n-1) bound is the headline comparator. It is attributed only by author name, with no `\\cite`. The Lovász survey is also named without a citation. A reviewer would expect both to be cited directly.\n- **Search topics:** Aleliunas–Karp–Lipton–Lovász–Rackoff, \"Random walks, universal traversal sequences, and the complexity of maze problems\"; Lovász, \"Random walks on graphs: a survey\".\n\n### 2. discussion-resistance-matthews-features (medium, positioning)\n- **Section:** Discussion and Limitations (the Conclusion repeats the point)\n- **Anchor:** \"resistance-based quantities motivated by the commute-time identity and Matthews-type bounds\"\n- **Reason:** The paper's main proposed next step, richer resistance-based features, rests on the commute-time identity and Matthews bounds. Neither has any support in the draft or the bibliography.\n- **Search topics:** Chandra et al., electrical resistance and commute/cover times; Matthews bounds on covering times via maximum hitting time.\n\n### 3. method-ridge-regression-background (low, background)\n- **Section:** Related Work (\"Statistical prediction with cheap features\")\n- **Anchor:** \"Predicting a graph functional from low-dimensional summaries is a standard use of regression\"\n- **Reason:** The paper claims no novelty for the model class, so a single classic ridge reference suffices.\n- **Search topics:** Hoerl and Kennard, \"Ridge regression: biased estimation for nonorthogonal problems\".\n\n## Deliberately not targeted\n- Quantitative claims, the evaluation protocol and the closed-form cover times are grounded in `paper_data.json` and the repository scripts.\n- Broader literatures were skipped (cover times of random regular or Erdős–Rényi graphs, learned graph-property prediction). Covering them would reshape the related-work story rather than fill a thin spot.\n# Paper Strategy (paper_standalone, ICLR 2026, double-blind)\n\n**Status:** ready (narrow, measurement-style paper; no improvement claim).\n\n## Title (provisional)\nHow Far Can Five Cheap Graph Statistics Go? Log-Linear Cover-Time Prediction on Small Connected Graphs\n\n## Central question\nFor connected graphs with 6<=n<=30, does a ridge model on cheap statistics (n, m, mean degree, max degree, diameter) predict Monte Carlo start-averaged cover time with lower family-macro log error than the generic 2m(n-1) bound?\n\n## Central thesis\nOn the sealed held-out graphs, the five-statistic log-linear ridge predictor (family-macro log MAE 0.181) is far closer to Monte Carlo cover times than the 2m(n-1) bound (1.754), a constant control (0.870) and a label-shuffled control (1.423); median Monte Carlo relative SE is about 0.029. Scope is 12 synthetic families, 24 test graphs. Which families the predictor fails on is *not* answered by the retained evidence.\n\n## Why this framing\n- The frozen selection is the seed baseline (`study.selection_kind=baseline`, investigation `completed_no_winner`, 1 attempt, 0 scored, 1 unscored, search delta 0.0). The studied method *is* the baseline, so no \"improvement over baseline\" is claimed. The comparison that exists is against the 2m(n-1) bound and controls, all in the same sealed final run.\n- Final outcome is `qualified` / `completed` / correct=true (`final.qualification.*`). Candidate diagnostics trust is `unverified`; disclose. No failed gates exist to discuss.\n- The unscored resistance/Matthews variant has