{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Bqr5p7UWVaZubvBkPY7zyo","type":"tool_result","content":"67:   \"claim\": \"Worst-case concentration: no family mean log error exceeds 12 x 0.181 = 2.18 (factor about 8.8) and no single test graph exceeds 24 x 0.181 = 4.35 (factor about 78) (derived arithmetic).\",\n119:   \"claim\": \"Protocol facts (36 training graphs, 24 test graphs, 12 families, about 300 walks, clip 10, tolerance 1e-6, seeds 201-203) describe the setting in which the sealed family-macro log MAE is computed; derived from protocol_inputs/ via protocol_facts.json.\",\n['final.evidence.episodes', 'final.metric.bound_macro_log_mae', 'final.metric.combined_score', 'final.metric.const_macro_log_mae', 'final.metric.improvement_vs_bound', 'final.metric.macro_log_mae', 'final.metric.mc_rel_se_median', 'final.metric.perm_max_rel_dev', 'final.metric.shuffle_macro_log_mae', 'final.qualification.correct', 'final.qualification.outcome', 'final.qualification.status', 'result.total_attempts', 'result.total_correct_programs', 'result.total_evaluated_programs', 'result.total_ideas', 'study.attempts.scored', 'study.attempts.total', 'study.attempts.unscored', 'study.baseline_score', 'study.final.correct', 'study.final.outcome', 'study.final.status', 'study.investigation_outcome', 'study.search_score', 'study.search_score_delta', 'study.selection_kind']\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/closed_form_gap.pdf\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[closed_form_gap.json]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/closed_form_gap.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n\\paragraph{Training-set size.} The model is fitted on $36$ labelled graphs (three per family) with five features of rank four; the ridge penalty $\\lambda=1$ is a fixed constant. Whether the result is sensitive to $\\lambda$, to training-set size or to the composition of the training families was not measured.\n\n\\paragraph{Feature attribution and ceiling.} We do not know which features carry the gain, nor how close a function of these five statistics can get to the label: structurally different graphs can share all five statistics. Richer invariants (for instance, effective-resistance quantities motivated by the relation between commute times and resistances \\citep{Doyle1984RandomWA,Lovasz2001RandomWO}) are natural candidates, but a configuration of that kind produced no score in this study and we draw no conclusion about it.\n\n\\paragraph{Range of validity.} All graphs have $n\\le 30$ and are drawn from twelve named families; labels are start-averaged, not worst-case-start, cover times. Extrapolation to larger graphs, to other families (e.g.\\ scale-free or hyperbolic random graphs \\citep{Kiwi2022CoverAH}) or to the maximum over start vertices is untested. A power-law fit calibrated at $n\\le 30$ has no guarantee at larger $n$ and, in particular, should not be expected to respect the extremal exponents known from \\citet{Feige1995ATU}.\n\n\\paragraph{Evaluation scope and label noise.} Per-graph Monte Carlo standard errors were not retained, so the noise on the slowest-covering graphs cannot be assessed here, and the clipped log error saturates at $10$, which would hide very large failures. Per-graph errors were not retained, so whether any test graph approaches that cap cannot be checked here.\n\n\\paragraph{Summary of what is and is not established.} Table~\\ref{tab:status} separates the statements that the evidence supports from those it does not.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0b64f47-5745-4f15-9f3f-c99e00f2da06","uuid":"d0dba21b-43e6-4052-9398-09cc5e185c35","timestamp":"2026-10-27T17:56:46.114Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"67:   \"claim\": \"Worst-case concentration: no family mean log error exceeds 12 x 0.181 = 2.18 (factor about 8.8) and no single test graph exceeds 24 x 0.181 = 4.35 (factor about 78) (derived arithmetic).\",\n119:   \"claim\": \"Protocol facts (36 training graphs, 24 test graphs, 12 families, about 300 walks, clip 10, tolerance 1e-6, seeds 201-203) describe the setting in which the sealed family-macro log MAE is computed; derived from protocol_inputs/ via protocol_facts.json.\",\n['final.evidence.episodes', 'final.metric.bound_macro_log_mae', 'final.metric.combined_score', 'final.metric.const_macro_log_mae', 'final.metric.improvement_vs_bound', 'final.metric.macro_log_mae', 'final.metric.mc_rel_se_median', 'final.metric.perm_max_rel_dev', 'final.metric.shuffle_macro_log_mae', 'final.qualification.correct', 'final.qualification.outcome', 'final.qualification.status', 'result.total_attempts', 'result.total_correct_programs', 'result.total_evaluated_programs', 'result.total_ideas', 'study.attempts.scored', 'study.attempts.total', 'study.attempts.unscored', 'study.baseline_score', 'study.final.correct', 'study.final.outcome', 'study.final.status', 'study.investigation_outcome', 'study.search_score', 'study.search_score_delta', 'study.selection_kind']\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/closed_form_gap.pdf\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[closed_form_gap.json]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/closed_form_gap.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n\\paragraph{Training-set size.} The model is fitted on $36$ labelled graphs (three per family) with five features of rank four; the ridge penalty $\\lambda=1$ is a fixed constant. Whether the result is sensitive to $\\lambda$, to training-set size or to the composition of the training families was not measured.\n\n\\paragraph{Feature attribution and ceiling.} We do not know which features carry the gain, nor how close a function of these five statistics can get to the label: structurally different graphs can share all five statistics. Richer invariants (for instance, effective-resistance quantities motivated by the relation between commute times and resistances \\citep{Doyle1984RandomWA,Lovasz2001RandomWO}) are natural candidates, but a configuration of that kind produced no score in this study and we draw no conclusion about it.\n\n\\paragraph{Range of validity.} All graphs have $n\\le 30$ and are drawn from twelve named families; labels are start-averaged, not worst-case-start, cover times. Extrapolation to larger graphs, to other families (e.g.\\ scale-free or hyperbolic random graphs \\citep{Kiwi2022CoverAH}) or to the maximum over start vertices is untested. A power-law fit calibrated at $n\\le 30$ has no guarantee at larger $n$ and, in particular, should not be expected to respect the extremal exponents known from \\citet{Feige1995ATU}.\n\n\\paragraph{Evaluation scope and label noise.} Per-graph Monte Carlo standard errors were not retained, so the noise on the slowest-covering graphs cannot be assessed here, and the clipped log error saturates at $10$, which would hide very large failures. Per-graph errors were not retained, so whether any test graph approaches that cap cannot be checked here.\n\n\\paragraph{Summary of what is and is not established.} Table~\\ref{tab:status} separates the statements that the evidence supports from those it does not.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
