{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTB2Mj5CQE4QSG6PwpdW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YAZuV18qsGTRxhLRa2GYPA","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np='paper.tex'\ns=open(p).read()\na=\"the bound $2m(n-1)$ is attained up to constant factors only by bottlenecked graphs such as paths and lollipops, and on dense or well-connected graphs it overshoots by a factor that grows with $n$ (Section~\\\\ref{sec:rationale}).\"\nb=\"the bound $2m(n-1)$ is tight up to a constant factor on some graphs such as paths and cycles, and on dense or well-connected graphs it overshoots by a factor that grows with $n$ (Section~\\\\ref{sec:rationale}).\"\nassert a in s; s=s.replace(a,b)\na2=\"and the clipped log error saturates at $10$, which would hide very large failures. Per-graph errors were not retained, so whether any test graph approaches that cap cannot be checked here.\"\nb2=\"and per-graph and per-family errors were not retained. The clip at $10$ on the per-graph log error cannot have been binding: the family-macro value of $0.181$ bounds every single test graph's error at about $4.35$ (a factor of about $78$).\"\nassert a2 in s; s=s.replace(a2,b2)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nimport json\nm=json.load(open('figure_manifest.json'))\nm['figures'][2]['source_ids']=['final.metric.bound_macro_log_mae']\njson.dump(m,open('figure_manifest.json','w'),indent=2)\nEOF\ngrep -n \"lollipop\" paper.tex | head; grep -n \"ln 4\\|ln\\s*4\" paper.tex | head -3"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1640,"cache_read_input_tokens":42861,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1640,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0b64f47-5745-4f15-9f3f-c99e00f2da06","uuid":"f0966048-cb42-41b1-9e49-ab8866259129","timestamp":"2026-10-27T17:56:53.808Z","request_id":"req_011CgTB2LxBpWueEYiQLHf5q"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YAZuV18qsGTRxhLRa2GYPA","type":"tool_result","content":"60:\\paragraph{Worst-case bounds.} The $2m(n-1)$ bound of Aleliunas et al.\\ \\citep{Aleliunas1979RandomWU} follows by summing commute times, each at most $2m$, over the $n-1$ edges of a spanning tree. Feige's upper bound, $(4/27+o(1))n^3$, is attained by lollipop-like graphs, and his companion lower bound, $(1+o(1))\\,n\\ln n$, is attained by the complete graph \\citep{Feige1995ATU,Feige1995ATL}. Together these delimit the possible range of cover times for a given $n$ but are worst-case over all connected graphs; they are not intended as point predictions for a given graph. Lov\\'asz's survey on random walks on graphs \\citep{Lovasz2001RandomWO} collects the hitting-time and commute-time machinery behind such bounds, and the electrical-network view of random walks \\citep{Doyle1984RandomWA} relates commute times to effective resistances. Our comparison against $2m(n-1)$ therefore measures how much a worst-case guarantee costs as a predictor on small graphs; it is not a criticism of the bound, which remains valid.\n111:\\paragraph{Why family-macro aggregation.} The score averages graphs within a family first and then averages the $12$ family means with equal weight. This prevents a family with more or larger graphs from dominating the score and puts the structurally extreme families (path, lollipop, barbell) on the same footing as benign ones. The price is variance: with two test graphs per family, each family mean is a mean of two terms, and the score is sensitive to individual graphs.\n115:\\paragraph{Graphs and labels.} Graphs are synthesised by code from twelve families: path, cycle, star, complete, grid, complete bipartite, barbell, lollipop, binary tree, random tree, Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi, and random regular. The sealed evaluation uses $36$ labelled training graphs ($3$ per family, $6\\le n\\le 30$) and $24$ test graphs ($2$ per family, $8\\le n\\le 26$). % @evidence[[protocol_facts.json#final_train]] @evidence[[protocol_facts.json#final_test]]\n191:\\paragraph{Small test set and composition.} Each family contributes only two test graphs, and families are weighted equally regardless of size or variability. A single mis-predicted graph shifts its family mean by half of that graph's error and the macro mean by one twenty-fourth of it. The test set includes extremal families (lollipop, barbell, path) as well as well-connected ones (complete, random regular), which is useful for contrast, but the sample is not a draw from any natural population of graphs. The result describes this mixture and nothing else.\n78:Figure~\\ref{fig:gap} plots the resulting log overshoot $\\ln[2m(n-1)/C]$. On the path it is essentially constant, about $0.54$ at $n=30$; on the cycle it is exactly $\\ln 4\\approx1.39$ at every $n$; on the star it grows like $\\ln(n/\\ln n)$ to about $2.0$ at $n=30$; and on the complete graph it grows like $\\ln(n^2/\\ln n)$ to about $5.4$ at $n=30$. % @evidence[[closed_form_gap.json#log_overshoot]]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0b64f47-5745-4f15-9f3f-c99e00f2da06","uuid":"7e6c503a-671f-425f-8e00-a935582a4edc","timestamp":"2026-10-27T17:56:53.945Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"60:\\paragraph{Worst-case bounds.} The $2m(n-1)$ bound of Aleliunas et al.\\ \\citep{Aleliunas1979RandomWU} follows by summing commute times, each at most $2m$, over the $n-1$ edges of a spanning tree. Feige's upper bound, $(4/27+o(1))n^3$, is attained by lollipop-like graphs, and his companion lower bound, $(1+o(1))\\,n\\ln n$, is attained by the complete graph \\citep{Feige1995ATU,Feige1995ATL}. Together these delimit the possible range of cover times for a given $n$ but are worst-case over all connected graphs; they are not intended as point predictions for a given graph. Lov\\'asz's survey on random walks on graphs \\citep{Lovasz2001RandomWO} collects the hitting-time and commute-time machinery behind such bounds, and the electrical-network view of random walks \\citep{Doyle1984RandomWA} relates commute times to effective resistances. Our comparison against $2m(n-1)$ therefore measures how much a worst-case guarantee costs as a predictor on small graphs; it is not a criticism of the bound, which remains valid.\n111:\\paragraph{Why family-macro aggregation.} The score averages graphs within a family first and then averages the $12$ family means with equal weight. This prevents a family with more or larger graphs from dominating the score and puts the structurally extreme families (path, lollipop, barbell) on the same footing as benign ones. The price is variance: with two test graphs per family, each family mean is a mean of two terms, and the score is sensitive to individual graphs.\n115:\\paragraph{Graphs and labels.} Graphs are synthesised by code from twelve families: path, cycle, star, complete, grid, complete bipartite, barbell, lollipop, binary tree, random tree, Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi, and random regular. The sealed evaluation uses $36$ labelled training graphs ($3$ per family, $6\\le n\\le 30$) and $24$ test graphs ($2$ per family, $8\\le n\\le 26$). % @evidence[[protocol_facts.json#final_train]] @evidence[[protocol_facts.json#final_test]]\n191:\\paragraph{Small test set and composition.} Each family contributes only two test graphs, and families are weighted equally regardless of size or variability. A single mis-predicted graph shifts its family mean by half of that graph's error and the macro mean by one twenty-fourth of it. The test set includes extremal families (lollipop, barbell, path) as well as well-connected ones (complete, random regular), which is useful for contrast, but the sample is not a draw from any natural population of graphs. The result describes this mixture and nothing else.\n78:Figure~\\ref{fig:gap} plots the resulting log overshoot $\\ln[2m(n-1)/C]$. On the path it is essentially constant, about $0.54$ at $n=30$